The function $f(x) = \dfrac{x - 2}{2\left(\sqrt{x - 1} - 1\right)} + \sqrt{x - 1}$ is given. Find:

a) its domain;

b) the values of $x$ for which $f(x) = 1$.

The expressions $A = \dfrac{1 + \sin\alpha + \cos\alpha}{\sqrt{2}\cos\frac{\alpha}{2}}$ and $B = 2\cos\left(45^\circ - \dfrac{\alpha}{2}\right)$ are given. For the admissible values of $\alpha$ prove that:

a) $A = B$;

b) $B = \dfrac{1 + \sqrt{7}}{2}$, if $\operatorname{tg}\dfrac{\alpha}{2} = \sqrt{7}$ and $\alpha \in (0^\circ;\ 180^\circ)$.
